---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/beauty';
const title = "Choosing Shopify email apps for beauty brands";
const desc = "Choose around routine education, product-specific replenishment, and the boundary between a one-time order and an active subscription. A useful app keeps those states separate without asking an operator to reconcile spreadsheets before every send.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which app should a beauty brand choose?",
    "answer": "Choose the candidate that demonstrates your product-size, education, subscription, and suppression rules with maintainable operator steps and a current quote."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I use one reorder delay for every product?",
    "answer": "Only if your evidence supports it. Size, quantity, purchase type, and usage uncertainty should inform the reminder and its wording."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should a skipped subscription start winback?",
    "answer": "Check the actual subscription state and the customer\u2019s chosen next step. A skip can mean excess stock rather than a lost customer."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/for">All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Write the product and routine brief</h2>
      <p>List the purchased SKU, size, delivery event, approved usage content, and whether the order is a sample, full-size purchase, gift, or subscription. A sample recipient needs a route to learn about the product; a repeat full-size buyer may need a reorder link rather than another introduction. Keep customer-selected preferences separate from assumptions inferred from a purchase.</p>
      <p>Ask the catalog owner to maintain the routine map. If a product is reformulated or its instructions change, record which templates and recommendations need review. Do not let the automation author infer ingredient compatibility or promise a result from a product name.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Capture a preference without diagnosing a customer</h2>
      <p>Offer a choice such as makeup shade updates, product education, or replenishment reminders. Explain what the choice changes and let a subscriber revise it. A preference for fragrance-free products is useful merchandising input; it does not establish a medical condition or permission for every channel.</p>
      <p>Send a sample-request confirmation through its designated owner. Marketing enrollment should have its own recorded state so that requesting a sample does not silently start every promotional sequence. Check that a corrected preference replaces the old value rather than leaving contradictory tags.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Build a replenishment matrix with uncertainty</h2>
      <p>Give each size and product its own proposed reminder window, based on actual reorder history where available. Record the source and uncertainty of that window. Quantity, shared use, travel, and alternate-day use can change depletion; an order date alone cannot tell you that a bottle is empty.</p>
      <p>Phrase the message as an option to reorder, not a claim that the customer has run out. Recheck for another order immediately before sending. If the same customer buys several sizes, define whether the newest purchase cancels the reminder or starts an independent product window.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Make billing state the subscription authority</h2>
      <p>The subscription system should own charge, skip, pause, and cancellation messages. The marketing operator needs a documented incoming state and a freshness check before enabling reorder promotions. A queued reminder must be reconsidered when a customer skips or changes quantity.</p>
      <p>A paused customer may simply have excess stock. Offer a useful route to manage the subscription and respect the chosen resume date. Cancellation should end the subscription-specific recovery path; any later marketing depends on the separate marketing status and the message purpose.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Teach the purchased product before adding another</h2>
      <p>Use the current approved product instructions as the source for a welcome-to-product sequence. Tie education to delivery when that event is available, and provide a fallback when delivery is unknown. Avoid turning a fulfillment delay into a reminder to start using an item that has not arrived.</p>
      <p>Customer questions about a reaction or suitability belong with the appropriate support or qualified reviewer. Pause routine cross-sell while that case is open. The email should link to the approved instructions and support route rather than generating personalized treatment advice.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Review bundles at the routine level</h2>
      <p>For each entry product, ask the product team which complements are appropriate and which recommendations should be excluded. Buying a bundle should suppress separate offers for products already inside it. Include a no-recommendation path when the purchase history is incomplete.</p>
      <p>Preserve the useful content even when there is no sale to make: storage guidance, application instructions, and how to find the correct refill. A discount is one possible offer, not the default next step for every customer who reads an education email.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Keep claim approval attached to the template</h2>
      <p>Assign an owner for product claims and save the approved wording, scope, and version alongside the email. A launch edit that adds a new promise should return to that owner before scheduling. This workflow is an editorial control, not a certification of legal compliance.</p>
      <p>Maintain a separate email and SMS eligibility record and a clear owner for suppression changes. Have the responsible privacy and legal reviewers determine applicable requirements for the store and markets. Do not treat an app badge or an integration connection as that determination.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Compare suppliers on the same beauty exercise</h2>
      <p>Ask Sequenzy, Klaviyo, Omnisend, Drip, or another candidate to demonstrate a sample-to-full-size path and a product-size reorder path using the same sanitized records. Include an active subscription, a late skip, a second order, and a preference correction. Save the actual field mappings and the operator steps required.</p>
      <p>If capture is the gap, include Privy or your existing form tool in the exercise and check how preferences and suppressions reach the sender. Request a current written quote for contacts, planned messages, SMS destinations, integrations, and support. These are demonstration requests, not claims that every named supplier passes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Move one routine with a reversible handoff</h2>
      <p>Export suppression and preference records before the move, reconcile identities, and choose one hero product for the first migration. Run the replacement in preview while the incumbent remains the sole live sender. Pause its corresponding trigger before enabling the replacement.</p>
      <p>Rehearse a late order, delayed subscription update, duplicate event, and withdrawal of marketing permission with staff accounts. Record expected recipients and actual output. If state cannot be trusted, stop the affected promotion and leave billing and support messages with their existing owners.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Measure repeat value without inventing a win</h2>
      <p>Report repeat orders by product and size, returns, subscription changes, unsubscribes, and contribution after discounts and messaging costs. Separate sample conversions from repeat full-size buyers so a sampling campaign does not masquerade as better replenishment.</p>
      <p>Choose a comparable cohort or a planned holdout if appropriate and allow purchases time to mature. A proposed reminder exercise establishes whether rules work; it does not establish revenue lift. Review content and frequency by preference rather than promising one promotion ratio for every beauty list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Keep the native baseline in the comparison</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify documents Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email</a>, for campaigns and marketing automations. Compare your actual requirements with the setup available to your store before deciding to replace it. The baseline does not establish that a particular edge case works; demonstrate that case with your own field mapping.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Build a selection brief</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">plan the migration</a>, and <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">review incentive costs</a>. Keep the failed requirement, responsible owner, and proposed next step in the decision record.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Store email questions" />
</BaseLayout>
